          %h2#Idea Idea
          %p
            In the current
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+particles"} fundamental particles
            in the three
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generations+of+fermions"} generations of fermions
            have identical properties from one generation to the next, except for their
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mass"} mass
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lepton"} lepton
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flavour+physics"} flavour
              universality
            (LFU). A possible violation of lepton flavour universality (LFUV) is also called a
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flavour+in+particle+physics"} flavour
                anomaly
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model
          %p
            The presence – or not – of flavour anomalies is part of the general
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flavour+problem"} flavour problem
            of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
          %p
            Flavor universality pertains in particular to (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semileptonic+decay"}> semi
            \-)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/leptonic+decays"} leptonic decays
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bottom+quarks"} bottom quarks
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            (in the third
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generation+of+fundamental+particles"} generation
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electron"} electron
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/positron"} positron
            pairs, denoted
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi e
                    %mo +
                  %msup
                    %mi e
                    %mo −
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e^+ e^-
            (in the first
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generation+of+fundamental+particles"} generation
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/muon"} muon
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/antiparticle"} anti
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/muon"} muon
            pairs, denoted
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi μ
                    %mo +
                  %msup
                    %mi μ
                    %mo −
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu^+ \mu^-
            (in the second
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generation+of+fundamental+particles"} generation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℓ
                    %mo +
                  %msup
                    %mi ℓ
                    %mo −
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ell^+ \ell^-
            (for “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/leptons"}> leptons
            ”) in this context.
          %p
            Since
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quarks"} quarks
            never appear in isolation (due to
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/confinement"} confinement
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bound+states"} bound states
            to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hadrons"} hadrons
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitude"} decays
            specifically of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bottom+quarks"} bottom quarks
            manifest themselves in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/leptonic+decays"} leptonic decays
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mesons"} mesons
            that contain
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            –
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-mesons"} B-mesons
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            –
          %figure{:style => "margin: 0 0 0 0"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/bsllprocesses.jpg", :width => "570px"}/
            %figcaption{:style => "text-align: center"}
              from
              %a{:href => "#Cartelle18"} Cartelle 18
          %p
            or as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/leptonic+decays"} leptonic decays
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/baryons"} baryons
            that contain
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            –
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lambda+baryons"} Lambda baryons
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Λ
                    %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda_b
            (measured only more recently (
            %a{:href => "#LHCb191208139"}> LHCb 19120.8139
            )).
          .float_right_image{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 10px 20px"}
            %figure{:style => "margin: 0 0 0 0"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/AltmannshoferRKDecay.jpg", :width => "300px"}/
              %figcaption{:style => "text-align: center"}
                from
                %a{:href => "flavor+problem#Altmannshofer14"} Altmannshofer 14
          %p
            Hence flavour universality in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
            demands, for example, that the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ratios"} ratios
            of decay rates (branching fractions) of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-mesons"} B-mesons
            into
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electrons"} electrons
            are the same as into
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/muons"} muons
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ratio"} ratio
          #eq:RKInIntroduction.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi R
                    %mi K
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                      %mo →
                      %mi K
                      %msup
                        %mi μ
                        %mo +
                      %mi μ
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                      %mo →
                      %mi K
                      %msup
                        %mi e
                        %mo +
                      %msup
                        %mi e
                        %mo −
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  R_K
                  \\;=\;
                  \\frac{
                  B \to K \mu^+ \mu
                  }{
                  B \to K e^+ e^-
                  }
          %p
            (where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kaon"} kaon
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/meson"} meson
            containing a
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strange+quark"} strange quark
          %p
            is essentially equal to 1. Any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/statistical+significance"} significant
            departure of this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable"} observable
            from 1 would be a
            %em flavour anomaly
            and would mean that there are fundamental processes at play which are not described by the current
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
            (“New Physics”).
          %p
            Moreover, in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model
            the decays involved here are “forbidden” (have vanishing decay rate) at
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tree+level"} tree level
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/loop+orders"} loop orders
            contribute to any possible flavour anomaly. But these loop contributions are sensitive to effects from putative undiscovered
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+particles"} fundamental particles
            which “run in the loops” as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/virtual+particles"} virtual particles
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mass"} mass
            is too large for them to be seen as scattering products (i.e. as actual, non-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/virtual+particles"}> virtual particles
            ).
          %p#CleanObservablesInIntroduction
            Finally, in forming the “branching”
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ratios"} ratios
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:RKInIntroduction"} (1)
            it is expected (see e.g.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#NabeebaccusZwicky22"} Nabeebaccus &amp; Zwicky 2022
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+effects"} non-perturbative effects
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD"} QCD
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "confinement#OpenProblemConfinementReferences"} open problem of confinement
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#HillerKrueger04"} Hiller &amp; Krüger 2004
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#IsidoriEtAl21"} Isidori et al. 2021
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QED"} QED
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/radiative+corrections"} radiative corrections
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#BordoneIsidoriPattori16"} Bordone, Isidori and Pattori 2016
            %a{:href => "#CFFPSSV21"} CFFPSSV 2021
            for cautionary remarks). Therefore these branching ratios are regarded as “clean observables” in that their value, to relevant precision, is unambiguously predicted by the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
          %p
            This way, tests of lepton flavour universality through measurement of branching ratios like
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:RKInIntroduction"} (1)
            provide a means to potentially detect “New Physics” (effects not described by the present
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model
            %em indirect
            high-luminosity/high-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/precision+experiment"} precision experiment
            (see e.g.
            %a{:href => "#Brambilla14"} Brambilla et al. 2014, Section 5
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#AHRS20"} AHRS 2020
            %em direct
            detection measurements.
          %p
            Notice that the channel
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi R
                    %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R_K
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:RKInIntroduction"} (1)
            is just one among the more prominent of several channels in which flavour anomalies may and have been seen. There are other decay channels of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-mesons"} B-mesons
            such as with differing spectator quarks (
            %a{:href => "#LHCB21"}> LHCB 2021
            ) and there are also flavour anomalies in decays of, for instance,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kaons"} kaons
            (
            %a{:href => "kaon#Buras16"}> Buras 16
            ,
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "kaon#Buras18"} Buras 2018
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#LHCb2012"} LHCb 2020
          %p
            See
            %a{:href => "#Alonso19"} Alonso 2019
            for general introduction to an audience with basic background in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
          %h2#StatisticalSignificance Statistical significance
          %p
            While the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/statistical+significance"} statistical significance
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flavour+anomalies"} flavour anomalies
            in every single decay channel is about
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2\sigma
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 3
                  %mi σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 3\sigma
            (around
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 4
                  %mi σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 4 \sigma
            for some), the observations across channels are all compatible with each other, with a high joint/global statistical significance.
          %p
            %a{:href => "#Garisto20"}> Garisto 20
            \:
          %blockquote
            %p Alone, each notable B physics result is only a few-sigma discrepancy. But taken together, the aggregate of the results is—depending on whom you ask—a 5- to 7-sigma deviation from the standard model estimates.
          %p
            and quoting
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gino+Isidori"} Isodori
          %blockquote
            %p “We’ve seen a lot of anomalies here and there popping up and going back, but this time I think it’s different… . For the first time, it’s not just one thing that doesn’t fit with the other, but it’s a coherent set of things.”
          %p
            Moreover, there are other types of anomalies currently observed in the data (such as in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anomalous+magnetic+moment"} anomalous magnetic moment
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/muon"} muon
            and in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mass"} mass
            of the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/W-boson"} W-boson
            = succeed "-meson" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            %a{:href => "#RelationToOtherAnomalies"} below
            for more).
          %p
            It is expected that if the ongoing evaluation of the data of
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/LHC"} LHC
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/statistical+significance"} statistical significance
            of the effect in each decay channel separately is expected to have reached 5
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+deviations"} σ
            (
            %a{:href => "#CrivellinEtAl18"}> Crivellin et. al 18, p. 12
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Zupan19"} Zupan 19, 4.6
            %a{:href => "#HMSN21"} HMSN 21
            this is expected for Run 3 data) and hence conventionally count as detection of flavour anomaly (see
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "statistical+significance#ParticlePhysics"} here
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/LHC"} LHC
            %a{:href => "#LHCbCollaboration18"} LHCb Collaboration 18
            predicts a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/statistical+significance"} statistical significance
            between 6 and 10
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            by the year 2025 (
            %a{:href => "#LKLR19"}> LKLR 19, p. 12
            ).
          %h3#up_to_2019 Up to 2019
          %p
            Indications of flavour anomalies have been observed (first suggested in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Hiller02"} Hiller 2002
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Koppenburg07"} Koppenburg 2007
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Koppenburg21"} Koppenburg 21
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/statistical+significance"} statistical significance
            around
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+deviations"} σ
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B+meson"} B meson
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitude"} processes
            in various decay channels and by a number of independent
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiments"} experiments
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/LHCb+experiment"} LHCb experiment
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Belle+experiment"} Belle experiment
            and the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BaBar+experiment"} BaBar experiment
          %div{:style => "text-align: center"}
            %figure{:style => "margin: 0 0 0 0"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/FlavourAnomaliesByChannels.jpg", :width => "590px"}/
              %figcaption{:style => "text-align: center"}
                table grabbed from
                %a{:href => "#BGV19"} BGV 19
          .float_right_image{:style => "margin: 0px 10px 10px 0px"}
            %figure{:style => "margin: 0 0 0 0"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/FlavourAnomalyAt4Sigma.jpg", :width => "320px"}/
              %figcaption{:style => "text-align: center"}
                graphics grabbed from
                %a{:href => "#Dey18"} Dey 18
          %p
            The global (all channels and experiments combined) tension with the standard model by end of 2018 had
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/statistical+significance"} statistical significance
            around
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 4.1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 4.1
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+deviations"} σ
            (
            %a{:href => "#StrumiaEtAl17"}> Strumia et al. 17
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Cartelle18"} Cartelle 18, slide 22/25
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Dey18"} Dey 18, slide 16/23
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#HAMN18"} HAMN 18, p. 4
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#BouchardCaoOwen19"} Bouchard-Cao-Owen 19
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 5
                  %mi σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 5 \sigma
            (
            %a{:href => "#CCDGMV17"}> CCDGMV 17
            ,
            %a{:href => "#CFMVV17"} CFMVV 17
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#CGMS18"} CGMS 18
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#Dordei18"} Dordei 18, slide 12
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "statistical+significance#ParticlePhysics"} here
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 5.3
                  %mi σ
                  %mo −
                  %mn 5.8
                  %mi σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 5.3 \sigma - 5.8 \sigma
            (
            %a{:href => "#ACDGMM19"}> ACDGMM 19
            ) or
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 5
                  %mo −
                  %mn 6
                  %mi σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 5-6 \sigma
            (
            %a{:href => "#KumarLondon19"}> Kumar-London 19
            ,
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Kumar19"} Kumar 19
          %p
            More recent measurements in March 2021 make even just the combination of the particularly “clean” observables reach
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 4
                    %mi σ
                    %mo −
                    %mn 4.6
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 4\sigma - 4.6\sigma
            %a{:href => "#SignificanceMarch2021"} below
            for more recent references.
          %h3#updates_in_2019 Updates in 2019
          %p
            This situation was confirmed with the completed measurements presented at
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "http://moriond.in2p3.fr/2019/"} Moriond 2019
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Straub19"} Straub 19
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Allanach19"} Allanach 19
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/statistical+significance"} statistical significance
            of the apparent anomaly essentially unaffected. But
            %a{:href => "#Caria19"} Caria 19, slide 9
            reported new measurements by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Belle+collaboration"} Belle
            (
            %a{:href => "#Belle1910"}> Belle 1910
            ) according to which the previous
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/statistical+significance"} statistical significance
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 3.8
                  %mi σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 3.8 \sigma
            in the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi R
                    %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R_D
            channel would decrease to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 3.1
                  %mi σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 3.1 \sigma
            or
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2.8
                  %mi σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2.8 \sigma
            (
            %a{:href => "V_cb+puzzle#GambinoJungSchacht19"}> Gambino-Jung-Schacht 19 (6)
            ). See
            %a{:href => "#LHCb19"} LHCb 19, p. 2
            for how the new numbers come about. The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/statistical+significance"} statistical significance
            over all sectors is still seen
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo &gt;
                  %mn 4
                  %mi σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gt 4 \sigma
            (
            %a{:href => "#London19"}> London 19, p. 7
            ) or
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∼
                  %mn 3
                  %mi σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sim 3\sigma
            (
            %a{:href => "#JaiswalNandiPatra20"}> Jaiswal-Nandi-Patra 20
            ). Moreover, the theoretical prediction is being corrected further away from these measured values (
            %a{:href => "#BDKL20"}> BDKL 20, footnote 1
            ).
          %p
            Comprehensive assessments of the situation after
            %a{:href => "http://moriond.in2p3.fr/2019/"} Moriond 2019
            are given in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#AHMSN19"} AHMSN 19
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#DescotesGenon19"} Descotes-Genon 19
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#BardhanGhosh19"} Bardhan-Ghosh 19
            %a{:href => "#Alguero19"} Alguero 19
            and agree that the flavour anomalies have been confirmed:
          %blockquote
            %p It appears the reason is that, given the amount of independent measurements (~180!) a smaller deviation because of new physics is easier to accommodate than a large one. Thus the new measurements actually fit better with new physics.
          %blockquote
            %p
              (Axel Maas, reporting from
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://indico.cern.ch/event/757995/"} ALPS2019
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://twitter.com/axelmaas/status/1120653147947655168"} tweet, 23 Apr 2019
          %div{:style => "text-align: center"}
            %figure{:style => "margin: 0 0 0 0"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/ZupanFlavourAnomaly.jpg", :width => "690px"}/
              %figcaption{:style => "text-align: center"}
                from
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "#Zupan19"} Zupan 19
                %a{:href => "#LHCb19"} LHCb19, Fig. 5
          %p
            There is the claim (
            %a{:href => "#DGKV19"}> DGKV 19
            ) that the discrepancy with the standard model increases further if non-trivial
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hadron"} hadronic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/form+factors"} form factors
            are taken into account:
          %p
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/DescotesGenonBsll19FormfactorCorrectionA.jpg", :width => "600px"}/
          %figure{:style => "margin: 0 0 0 0"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/DescotesGenonBsll19FormfactorCorrectionB.jpg", :width => "600px"}/
            %figcaption{:style => "text-align: center"}
              from
              %a{:href => "#DescotesGenon19b"} Descotes-Genon 19b
          %p
            and also that the discrepancy increases further with
            = succeed "-corrections" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QED"} QED
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#MishraMahajan20"} Mishra-Mahajan 20,p. 20-21
          %h3#updates_in_2020 Updates in 2020
          %p
            %a{:href => "#Smith20"} Smith 20
            on behalf of
            %a{:href => "#LHCb200304831"} LHCb 2003.04831
            (see also
            = succeed "," do
